            <ArticleTitle>Experience with vitamin E in coronary disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>391-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Fifty-four patients with various classifications of angina pectoris were treated with vitamin E in a study to evaluate the effectiveness of the substance in such conditions. The incidence of improvement among patients in the series was no greater than it was in a control group not given vitamin E. As to subjective evidence of relief, there was no significant difference as between patients given vitamin E and those given placebos. When some of the patients being treated with vitamin E were given placebos, instead, for a period during the course of observation, subjective improvement was not diminished. No specific benefits attributable to vitamin E therapy could be shown.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
